17592186049723 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1031 "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1033 1904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049723 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving 3x3 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1033 1374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "340f0f9d-19b0-5cff-abb5-75d2e7e75aa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Dependence and Independence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1033 1861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050426 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1031 "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1033 1864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050638 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119626 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119626 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ![](images/39/39c738068392d3e8f34d72cb46d270e480067d9db8b0353ba1eb05f3a961030c.svg) and ![](images/53/53b09ea4cefbe48a6e1690bc772e74bd59f361bfbc6410a1ae02ac3555233b23.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119626 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/ca/ca8ec71c9d54982494530c4458f3a596a3b61a0529acd42c9ddae3fe9c500e34.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119672 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119672 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ![](images/7d/7d5c2f2aaa1ee01159856bcfad7ef1039c169c2fdb96c9c4ab17db5ba9be9067.svg) ![](images/78/784f5d018101808c55cf63b332ae50c9d4fc8e3ded23ad9144e417052d2eaba3.svg) and ![](images/49/491d6bb66fd54ae64e406eef6607526c23c6451a3ed0df837d63170c0b9adb95.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119672 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/fd/fd0c93483a1a4386dd0269ac11cbe99567176e1e9d5dbd53477e46cb0704074c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119673 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119673 #edb/ref 1071 "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n![](images/3f/3ff69817bd8aeb5d8326b7ea9fdc68ceeef52840d4f77ee7dffb9f72514f4532.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119673 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n![](images/79/79525b86cbbbc191f9a19ffa817fdaebeb0d1f6bd99eb97ce3f642a7fdf6033e.svg)\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n![](images/c7/c732a274f27d98231f9a94dad40ff15f53e82f00b076ba46464ac640c2765703.svg)\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119815 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119857 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119858 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Basis of a Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "01aaa671-aae7-587d-86f6-117d15d3fbee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1061 17504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be88e95e-9faa-5bfe-b721-4eda45231dc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1061 17505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1169ef7f-a019-5cfa-8ef6-1d2ff514f2f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1061 23378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dee9e822-2a94-59f6-9320-08d94fcf8912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1061 23379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19b9d2e4-cc12-5198-97ee-5424e005fc32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1061 23546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fd42240-5623-532c-921e-a37910ca748c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1061 23547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1cd2039-adf1-5e16-9c01-414506e00c8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1064 "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n![](images/aa/aaa76e82d8d54b305cfcfb0414f1fe7dd4d1c9d3ff932b4206a7b864922b394e.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n![](images/23/237305d1b82f44e0c365f721a33c35d8d983bd5facd4471d1885e8180ec7267e.svg)\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0ff277adbace09315a1c0cd7c964eac41f5e2f080d0e32f888f379be435da0.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n![](images/79/79c529aa2fb5373ff9f8798300b1423ea63f90778bd4832ba1276f6af9c21f04.svg)\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1065 3206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ff21f5d-5007-5b74-b135-bdb080fd5541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1064 "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n![](images/4c/4c12a6e56a6cc7cd72371c59f41b33d8876cf4b98aa40766f0e67ad1bc3a32f7.svg)\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/1d/1dd0ca9bae9a29dea8fc34ed3d9fc453a1d52c48f1f19a95c2fb16c65cd9c7d8.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1065 3945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2c0f5ce4-8098-51b1-a25e-8cb4d84505c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Basis by Identifying Pivot Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n![](images/94/944c607bf892f0d346df79dfa08e6bfec699e64feac018d40a733be0a5c11041.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n![](images/70/70a26164bd64f6e4483b38a08187cac2e1498df7f9066e2be9a295e7b04459da.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/2a/2a92f6d5b67012d342685436d6ccc0ae1670791a060129b10c7d858cafda7b26.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/4d/4defcc004e360ec2e87798b158616575f284ae36369c766ee950c20ae06eff0a.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/1b/1be8cce3195aa633e76e855eba391dd8d1342cf0f3a19e0302eaf67e2828b909.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n![](images/cf/cf5185386d46084579b56fc6ad00769c7c5e7a0d2f320be7ccb28aa4961519ae.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1065 3968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "14b13853-b5c6-5bc9-825c-a11cd19dff93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315186 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bf09390-0935-56eb-bfeb-02365617f6a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315318 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315318 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23cfbcca-d1c9-580f-a79e-c