ed}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "585db78b-56e0-58b0-a767-e41e96247676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342844 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342844 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342844 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "499ee1f6-801f-57f7-aab7-474f9d374084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343334 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343334 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343334 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f5bfdd1-ee64-5fd7-9c47-3b4c4e1a2370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343425 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89079bb4-c6f6-5b1d-be0d-68fe5c1822de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343791 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343791 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343791 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1061a87-039d-51d8-ba91-e57582fc2d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344186 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de0e2b5b-97e2-5bfc-aeeb-9c68939e0ca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344919 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3d7b3da-d4d8-5800-9823-61684cbd00d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a38bfe9-93f0-569e-b890-07afd16179a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345006 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "500f0d4b-f4d9-5169-9a06-c653e34353e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386585 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186386587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386586 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "17aaf56d-891f-5d77-bb67-315f843fe760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386587 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386587 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386587 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2764e265-a9e8-5a46-82cd-5cdf0cd96f2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386588 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned